            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Superconductivity Research</strong><br>\nBardeen's next significant work was in the field of superconductivity, which describes the behavior of certain materials that can conduct electricity with no resistance at very low temperatures. His research led to the formulation of the BCS theory, named after Bardeen, Leon Cooper, and Robert Schrieffer.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Nobel Prize</strong><br>\nFor his pioneering work on superconductivity, John Bardeen, along with Cooper and Schrieffer, was awarded his second Nobel Prize in Physics in 1972. He became the only person to have won the Nobel Prize in Physics twice, a testament to his monumental contributions to science and engineering.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contribution to Science and Technology</strong><br>\nBardeen's contributions had a profound impact on technology and scientific understanding. His work on the transistor paved the way for the development of all modern electronics, including computers, smartphones, and other digital devices.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
