          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Life at the University of Illinois</strong><br>\nAfter his time at Bell Labs, Bardeen joined the faculty at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign. Here, he continued his research in solid-state physics and spent the remainder of his academic career nurturing future physicists and contributing to the field's expansion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nJohn Bardeen was known for his modesty and simple lifestyle, despite his fame. He was married to Jane Maxwell and was a father to three children. He enjoyed playing golf and was an avid reader. Bardeen's legacy is carried on by the numerous awards and lectureships established in his name.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nBardeen passed away on January 30, 1991. His death marked the loss of one of the greatest physicists of the 20th century. Posthumously, he has been recognized through the naming of institutes and scholarships, ensuring that his contributions to science will always be remembered.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
